    "## Prototype Architecture: ConvLSTM + UNet:\n",
    "\n",
    "**U-Net** - Spatial Feature Extraction.\n",
    "**ConvLSTM** - Spatiotemporal Memory.\n",
    "\n",
    "### U-Net (Spatial Specialist):\n",
    "Originally designed for image segmentation, U-Net treats the entire sequence of past radar frames as a single, multi-channel image (like an RGB image, but with time channels).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **How it works:** Downsampling the radar grid (AutoEncoder) to extract deep spatial patterns (fronts, cell shapes), then upsampling it back to the original resolution to output the future frame.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Drawbacks:** U-Net doesn't explicitly understand **time**. It just sees a stack of images and tries to map them to another stack of images with feature extraction.\n",
    "\n",
    "### ConvLSTM (The Temporal Specialist):\n",
    "\n",
    "ConvLSTM is an RNN where the internal matrix operations are replaced by 2D convolutions.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **How it works:** It processes one radar frame at a time. It updates a **hidden state** (memory) that tracks how the storm is moving and evolving through time. It passes this memory forward to predict subsequent frames.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Drawbacks:** RNNs are notoriously data hungry, hard to train, computationally expensive and also suffer from vanishing gradient problem over long sequences. \n",
    "\n",
    "### ConvLSTM + U-Net Architecture: \n",
    "\n",
    "Standard U-Net lacks temporal awareness and standard ConvLSTM struggles with spatial blurring, so we can use both to maximise the prediction power for nowcasting. Architectures like **SmaAT-UNet (Attention U-Net)** and **ConvLSTM variants** are strong practical choices.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The U-Net encoder compresses the massive radar grid into a smaller, dense feature map.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The ConvLSTM processes this compressed map over time to predict the future feature map.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The U-Net decoder expands it back into a high-resolution radar prediction."
